; A wide floor built almost entirely out of #18's crumbling ledge, all at the
; same height so no side of it is the "high ground" -- four 160x24 ledges
; strung out with 32-40 px gaps between them and the island, narrower than a
; 48 px body, so crossing is a matter of keeping moving rather than of jumping.
; Standing still is the mistake: whichever ledge a player parks on starts its
; warning the moment they land on it, and 1 s later it is gone for three
; seconds. With two players loose on the same floor, that clock is always
; running on somebody.
;
; Island is the one permanent piece, centred and deliberately small -- room
; for four to stand and not much more. It was 140 px, room for two; #36
; widened it to 216 so all four spawns fit on it, which narrows its gaps to
; the inner ledges to 32 px -- still too narrow for a body to drop through.
; It is not a balance choice: it is what keeps this stage from being able to
; erode to nothing under every player at once and deadlock the round with no
; floor left anywhere. Every spawn sits on top of it rather than on a ledge, so a
; round never opens with a countdown already ticking on the ground a player
; was just placed on (US-7) -- the erosion starts only once a player leaves
; home and stands on the wide floor built to fall out from under them.
;
; Eased in #59. The ledges were 200 px (centres +-240 and +-480) with
; CrumblingLedge's default 0.8 s warning, so getting from the island out to
; an outer ledge meant covering 240 px of ledge-plus-gap in 0.8 s -- 300 px/s,
; which only the staff and pickaxe sustain on flat ground at their very best
; (measured: ~300-315 px/s; sword ~200, dagger ~210, axe ~120). Now the ledges
; are 160 px (centres +-220 and +-420, the gaps unchanged) and warn for 1.0 s:
; 200 px in 1 s, so the pickaxe has room to spare and the sword and dagger can
; make it with a clean run. 1.0 s is also as long as the warning can go while
; the island sweep still sees every ledge fall in its window. The 3 s away is
; unchanged, so a lost ledge is still not simply waiting to be walked back
; onto; the floor is 160 px narrower overall, still well inside the camera.

; Backdrop (issue #117): desert.
[node name="Erosion" type="Node2D"]
